{"name":"Crow Feather","hex":"#102329","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bird, usually black, of the genus Corvus, having a strong conical beak, with projecting bristles; it has a harsh, croaking call.","Any of various dark-coloured nymphalid butterflies of the genus Euploea."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Dark black, the color of a crow; crow-black."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crow"},{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crow","blocks":[{"text":"A crow is a bird of the genus Corvus, or more broadly, a synonym for all of Corvus. The word \"crow\" is used as part of the common name of many species. The related term \"raven\" is not linked scientifically to any certain trait but is rather a general grouping for larger-sized species of Corvus. The collective name for a group of crows is a \"murder\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Corvus_corone_-near_Canford_Cliffs%2C_Poole%2C_England-8.jpg/330px-Corvus_corone_-near_Canford_Cliffs%2C_Poole%2C_England-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crow"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feather"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Parts of a feather: VaneShaft, rachisBarbsAftershaft, afterfeatherQuill, calamus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParts_of_feather_modified.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior region of a contour feather from a rock dove, visualized using scanning electron microscopy. Interlocking barbules are clearly seen in the middle image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APigeon_down_SEM.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg/500px-Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail